Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — that’s enough to give a clear overview and next steps. I’ll cover (1) what you can and cannot deduct, (2) whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, (3) how to treat the home office, (4) the IRS forms you’ll use, and (5) a few open questions and recommended next steps.

1) What you can (likely) deduct
- Consulting (self-employment) expenses: Costs that are ordinary and necessary for your consulting work (e.g., supplies, advertising, business portion of phone/internet if used for business, unreimbursed expenses that relate to the consulting work) are deductible on Schedule C. If the $950 “unreimbursed expenses” are for consulting, include them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed expenses for your W‑2 employer, they are not deductible (TCJA suspension through 2025).
- Home office: If you use the 165 sq ft exclusively and regularly for your consulting, you can take a home-office deduction for the portion of your home used for business (see details below).
- Student loan interest: Interest you paid in 2024 on qualified student loans may be deductible (up to $2,500) as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1), subject to MAGI phaseouts. You reported $4,800 — only the interest paid during the tax year is relevant, and the maximum allowed deduction per year is $2,500 (subject to income limits).
- Charitable contribution: Your $1,400 to Habitat for Humanity is a deductible charitable contribution if you itemize and have substantiation (receipt). If you take the standard deduction instead, you don’t get a separate deduction for that gift.
- Interest income: The $1,780 (Chase) and $195 (Fidelity) are taxable interest. These will be reported on Schedule B if your total taxable interest + ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 (your total is $1,975, so yes — use Schedule B).
- Self-employment tax: Not a deduction from income, but you must pay self-employment (SE) tax on net self-employment earnings; half of SE tax is deductible as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).

2) Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- Head of Household standard deduction (tax year 2024): substantially larger than single. (If you want, I can confirm the exact current IRS amount for 2024.)
- Compare what you’d get if you itemize vs standard. From the items you listed, the only clear itemized amount is the $1,400 charitable gift (plus state/local taxes and mortgage interest or property tax if any — you didn’t list those). With only $1,400 in charitable gifts and no mortgage interest or large state taxes given, your itemized deductions will almost certainly be far lower than the HOH standard deduction. So it is very likely you should take the standard deduction.
- Exception: If you have substantial state income taxes or property taxes, mortgage interest, or other deductible expenses not listed here, itemizing could make sense. Provide those amounts if you want a precise comparison.

3) Home office — how to treat it (and quick math)
You may choose one of two methods for the business-use-of-home deduction for your consulting income:

A. Simplified method
- IRS allows $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 165 sq ft: 165 × $5 = $825 deduction.
- Easy and requires less recordkeeping.

B. Actual-expense method (likely better in your case)
- Business percentage = office sq ft / total sq ft = 165 / 780 ≈ 21.15%.
